      "question": "I have a dataset with hourly timestamps, class labels, and 150 time-step feature columns. Can you profile this data and summarize what we're working with?",
      "response": "Here's a profile of your dataset based on the tool results:\n\n---\n\n## Dataset Overview\n\n| Property | Value |\n|---|---|\n| **Rows** | 40 |\n| **Timestamps** | Hourly, from `2020-01-01 00:00:00` to `2020-01-02 15:00:00` |\n| **Feature columns** | 150 (`t_0` through `t_149`) |\n| **Label column** | `label` (values 1 and 2) |\n| **Missing values** | None detected (count = 40 on all sampled columns) |\n\n---\n\n## Feature Statistics (sampled at t_0, t_50, t_100, t_149)\n\n| Column | Mean | Std | Min | Max | CV |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `t_0` | \u22120.996 | 0.344 | \u22122.013 | \u22120.546 | \u22120.345 |\n| `t_50` | 0.581 | 0.638 | \u22120.658 | 1.416 | 1.098 |\n| `t_100` | 0.304 | 0.674 | \u22121.036 | 1.332 | 2.220 |\n| `t_149` | \u22120.979 | 0.309 | \u22121.899 | \u22120.566 | \u22120.315 |\n\n**Key observations:**\n\n1. **Wave-like structure across the 150 time-steps.** The early columns (`t_0`) and late columns (`t_149`) have similar negative means (\u2248 \u22121.0) and low spread (std \u2248 0.3), while the middle columns (`t_50`, `t_100`) have positive means and much higher variability (std \u2248 0.64\u20130.67). This is consistent with each row being a **single oscillatory signal** (a wave) sampled at 150 points \u2014 low/negative at the tails, peaking in the middle.\n\n2. **High variability in the mid-section.** The coefficient of variation rises from ~0.35 at the tails to over 2.2 at `t_100`, meaning the wave amplitude and phase differ substantially across the 40 rows. Different rows reach their peaks at different points in the 150-step window.\n\n3. **Two class labels (1 and 2).** The `label` column alternates between 1 and 2, suggesting a binary classification task \u2014 likely distinguishing between two types of waveforms.\n\n---\n\n## Seasonality\n\n| Column | Dominant Period | Strength | Basis |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `t_0` | 16 obs | 0.356 | autocorrelation |\n| `t_50` | 24 obs | 0.0 | frequency default |\n| `t_100` | 24 obs | 0.0 | frequency default |\n\nSeasonality is **weak to absent**. The `t_0` column shows a mild autocorrelation at a 16-hour lag (strength 0.36), but the mid-section columns show no meaningful seasonality (strength 0.0, defaulting to a 24-hour frequency). This makes sense: each row is an independent waveform sample, not a continuous time series in the column-wise direction.\n\n---\n\n## Anomalies\n\nThe anomaly detector (robust z-score, selected via synthetic injection F1) ran on `t_50` and found **0 anomalies** across the 40 rows. The detector was fully supported, so this is a clean \"no anomalies\" result rather than an abstention.\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\nYou have **40 hourly observations**, each containing a **150-point waveform** with a characteristic rise-and-fall shape. The data is clean (no missing values, no anomalies detected), weakly seasonal at best, and tagged with a **binary label** (1 or 2). The primary variation across rows is in the **amplitude and timing of the wave peak** in the mid-section of the 150-step window. This looks well-suited for a waveform classification task.",
